"},{"tags":["quantum-mechanics","quantum-spin","computational-physics","software","bells-inequality"],"owner":{"account_id":3964622,"reputation":137,"user_id":537547,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14GgmMyPAC9XhqyNJTUuTZ_lwZm9d4TcHGnBtdISQWA=k-s256","display_name":"Johan Thiborg-Ericson","link":"https://physics.stackexchange.com/users/537547/johan-thiborg-ericson"},"is_answered":true,"view_count":381,"closed_date":1754847578,"answer_count":2,"score":2,"last_activity_date":1754830617,"creation_date":1754690996,"last_edit_date":1754830617,"question_id":857275,"link":"https://physics.stackexchange.com/questions/857275/is-my-unit-test-an-accurate-analogy-for-the-bell-test","closed_reason":"Duplicate","title":"Is my unit test an accurate analogy for the Bell test?","body":"<p>As pointed out in the comments, it is not entirely clear how this question contributes something new that hasn’t already been clearly and concisely expressed many times on this site and many other places. All other explanations of the Bell test I have found are either misleading or require a non-negligible amount of mathematical understanding. I understand those explanations seem so simple to you physics people, it isn’t quite clear for you how my explanation is considerably simpler. I have spent a lot of time finding explanations of the Bell test that are so simple they can be conveyed to a physics interested software engineer in the industry. I too thought it would be easy at first, but so far I haven’t succeeded. I think it would be a good thing to have an explanation of the Bell test that is accessible to the vast group of programmers that don’t have the skills, energy or motivation to understand the many excellent pure mathematical explanations that are readily available out there, and I can only hope you agree.</p>
<p>At three separate occasions I have been trying to explain the Bell test to different software engineer colleagues using the different mathematical explanations available online, but I have failed all times because the math is too hard to engage them. Fortunately, you can trick a software engineer into learning math of any complexity if you frame it as a programming problem guarded by a unit test.</p>
<p>I've based the math in my unit test on David Mermin’s paper <a href="https://doi.org/10.1063/pt.hsjm.vbey" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Is the Moon There When Nobody Looks? Reality and the Quantum Theory</a> because I find the 50/50 requirement there neater and more intruging than the math from his original description. To pass the test, a factory pattern should be implemented that creates pairs of &quot;entangled&quot; objects. The unit test then runs a million trials to assert two key behaviours, quoted from the above paper:</p>
<ol>
<li>If one examines only those runs in which the switches have the same setting [...], then one finds that the lights always flash the same colors.</li>
<li>If one examines all runs, without any regard to how the switches are set [...], then one finds that the pattern of flashing is completely random. In particular, half the time the lights flash the same colors, and half the time different colors.</li>
</ol>
<p>Of course, the point of the test is that it is very unlikely to pass unless you implement the particles to engage in “spooky interactions at a distance”.</p>
<pre><code>
public class BellInequalityTests
{
    public enum MeasurementProperty
    {
        A,
        B,
        C
    }

    public enum Color
    {
        Red,
        Green
    }

    [Fact]
    public void DemonstratesBellInequality()
    {
        var factory = new EntangledParticleFactory();
        var random = new Random();

        int totalTrials = 1_000_000;
        int differentResultsCount = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i &lt; totalTrials; i++)
        {
            var (particle1, particle2) = factory.CreateEntangledParticles();

            var choice1 = (MeasurementProperty)random.Next(3);
            var choice2 = (MeasurementProperty)random.Next(3);

            Color color1 = particle1.Measure(choice1);
            Color color2 = particle2.Measure(choice2);

            if (choice1 == choice2)
            {
                Assert.Equal(color1, color2);
            }

            if (color1 != color2)
            {
                differentResultsCount++;
            }
        }

        double probabilityOfDisagreement = (double)differentResultsCount / totalTrials;

        Assert.InRange(probabilityOfDisagreement, 0.499, 0.501);
    }
}

</code></pre>
<p>Is this unit test an accurate analogy for the Bell test?</p>
